With expeditious development of enzymatic biosensors in the field of cancer diagnosis, various nanosystems have been developed to accomplish the signal amplification and facilitate the improvement of sensitivity and selectivity. Nevertheless, the environmental variabilities such as the change of temperature, pH, and solvents often result in the
decrease of enzyme activity, which is the major challenge for their bioapplications (Wang et al., 2017). In recent years, zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs), a subclass of MOFs which are synthesized by joining the central metal ions (e.g. \( \mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \) and \( \mathrm{Co}^{2+} \)) with four imidazole ligands (Navid et al., 2022), have received special attentions in biosensing fields due to their advantages of porosity, high stability, good biocompatibility and large surface area (Lee et al., 2015). These unique physical and chemical properties endow ZIFs with excellent load capacity (Hani et al., 2021), which especially lays a good foundation for enzyme stabilization (Maddigan et al., 2021). It has been found the enzyme-triggered nucleation can achieve not only the encapsulation of enzymes in ZIFs but also preserve the biological activity of enzymes even in harsh environments (Liang et al., 2019). Such symbiotic reinforcement effect between ZIFs and enzymes opens a new avenue for the construction of biosensors.
In nature, the chemical transformations tend to be accomplished in terms of cascade reactions in a confined space, such as nanoscale enzymes or micron cells (Chen et al., 2018). Inspired by this, various nanoreactors have been designed to improve the reaction efficiency and selectivity (Li et al., 2018a). It has been proved that the porous ZIF exoskeletons can act as good candidates of artificial nanoreactors by structural confinement (Chen et al., 2018). The biocatalytic cascades in ZIF nanoreactors show superior activities and operational stability compared with the reaction occurred in homogeneous phase (Chen et al., 2018). Therefore, the ZIF-based nanoreactors hold great potential in biosensing through combination with signal amplification strategies (Li et al., 2018b).
As a typical tumor biomarker, the high expression of miRNA is closely related to multiple cancers (Campuzano et al., 2014). Therefore, the development of accurate, sensitive and selective method for miRNA detection is of great significance. Herein, a label-free electrochemical biosensor is constructed using GOx-encapsulated ZIF-8 (GOx@ZIF-8) as nanoreactors coupled with 3D DNA walker for amplified detection of miRNA. As shown in Scheme 1, the GOx@ZIF-8 nanoreactor with high catalytic activity is obtained by rapid GOx-triggered nucleation of ZIF-8.
After immobilization of AuNPs on GOx@ZIF-8 via electrostatic adsorption, the 3D orbits of DNA walker (H1) are constructed via Au–S covalent bond. Upon the introduction of target miRNA-21, the 3D DNA walker is initiated. In brief, the target miRNA-21 first hybridizes with the toehold domain of H1, resulting in the open of hairpin by strand displacement reaction. Then, H2 (fuel DNAs) with G-rich fragment hybridizes with H1 and form the H1/H2 hybrid, leading to the displacement of target miRNA-21 to initiate another cycle. As a result, a large number of fuel DNAs are immobilized on the electrode. Owing to the G-rich fragment in H2, the horseradish peroxidase (HRP)-mimicking DNAzyme is formed after addition of hemin. The DPV is known as a powerful technique providing ultra-low limit of detection (Omid et al., 2012). In the confined space of ZIF-8 nanoreactors, the cascade catalysis system of GOx and G-quadruplex/hemin HRP-mimicking DNAzyme is constructed, where the \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) as an intermediate is generated with the oxidization of glucose catalyzed by GOx and subsequently decomposed by G-quadruplex/hemin HRP-mimicking DNAzyme for further oxidation of ABTS to obtain DPV signals. Therefore, this ZIF-8 nanoreactor-based electrochemical biosensor coupled with 3D DNA walker achieves sensitive and selective detection of miRNA.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Synthesis of ZIF-8 and GOx@ZIF-8 nanoreactors
The ZIF-8 was synthesized via a solvothermal method according to the reported work with minor modifications (Karimi et al., 2019). First, \( 5.7\,\mathrm{mL} \) of 2-methylimidazole \( (2.86\,\mathrm{mM}) \) was slowly added to \( 2.58\,\mathrm{mL} \) of zinc nitrate \( (0.29\,\mathrm{mM}) \). After vigorously stirring at room temperature for \( 12\,\mathrm{h} \), the precipitates were filtrated and washed with ethanol for three times before freeze-drying. For the preparation of GOx@ZIF-8 nanoreactors, \( 5.7\,\mathrm{mL} \) of 2-methylimidazole \( (2.86\,\mathrm{mM}) \) was slowly added to \( 2.58\,\mathrm{mL} \) of zinc nitrate \( (0.29\,\mathrm{mM}) \) containing \( 20\,\mathrm{mg}\,\mathrm{GOx} \). The mixture was vigorously stirred at room temperature for \( 12\,\mathrm{h} \). The formed GOx@ZIF-8 nanoparticles were filtrated and washed with deionized
water for three times before freeze-drying.
## 2.2. Construction of 3D orbits for DNA walker
Firstly, the AuNPs with the average diameter of \(15~\mathrm{nm}\) were synthesized. In brief, \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) of \(\mathrm{HAuCl_{4}}\) \((25~\mathrm{mM})\) was added to \(150~\mathrm{mL}\) of boiling sodium citrate solution \((2.2\;\mathrm{mM})\) under continuously magnetic stirring. When the solution color turned to red, another \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) of \(\mathrm{HAuCl_{4}}\) \((25\,\mathrm{mM})\) was added and reacted at \(90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) . After repeating the above procedure twice, the \(15~\mathrm{nm}\) AuNPs were collected by centrifugation at \(4500g\) and washed with deionized water for three times. To prepare the \(25\,\mathrm{nm}\) AuNPs, \(2\,\mathrm{mL}\) of sodium citrate \(\left(60\,\mathrm{mM}\right)\) and \(53\,\mathrm{mL}\) of deionized water were added to \(55~\mathrm{mL}\) of the prepared \(15~\mathrm{nm}\) AuNPs under magnetic stirring. The obtained mixture was heated to \(90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) followed by adding \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) of \(\mathrm{HAuCl_{4}}\) \((25\,\mathrm{mM})\) every \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) for a total of \(3~\mathrm{mL}\) . Then, the \(25~\mathrm{nm}\) AuNPs were concentrated by centrifugation at \(4500g\) and washed with deionized water for three times before storing at \(4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Wolfgang et al., 2007). To prepare the \(\mathrm{AuNPs/GOx}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8\) nanoreactors, \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) of \(25~\mathrm{nm}\) AuNPs \((1.2\ \mathrm{mM})\) was mixed with \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) of \(\mathrm{GOx}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8\) nanoreactors \(\mathrm{[10~mg/mL)}\) . After reaction for \(^{12\mathrm{~h~}}\) , the synthesized \(\mathrm{AuNPs/GOx}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8\) was collected by centrifugation at \(9000g\) for \(5\,\textrm{m i n}\) and washed with ultrapure water for three times. Finally, the purified \(\mathrm{AuNPs/GOx}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8\) was obtained via freeze-drying.
Before immobilizing \(\mathrm{AuNPs/GOx}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8\) on the electrode, the bare glassy carbon electrode (GCE) was polished with \(0.3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) and \(0.05~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) alumina slurries, respectively, followed by sonication in methanol and ultrapure water in turn twice. Then, the electrode was cleaned with ultrapure water and dried at the nitrogen \(\left(\mathrm{N}_{2}\right)\) atmosphere. After that, \(10~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(\mathrm{AuNPs/GOx}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8\) (1 mL/mL) was dropped on the GCE and then naturally dried at \(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(140\ \mathrm{min}\) . Finally, to construct the 3D DNA orbits via Au–S covalent interaction, \(5~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of thiol-labeled H1 (2 \(\upmu\mathrm{M})\) was dropped on the modified electrode, incubated for \(2\,\mathrm{~h~}\) and washed with pure water for three times. Subsequently, the resulting electrode was rinsed with ultrapure water and blocked with \(10~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of MCH \((1.0\ \mathrm{mM})\) for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) . Then, the electrode was cleaned with ultrapure water for future usage.
## 2.3. Extraction of total RNA from cells
First, the LO2, MCF-7 and HeLa cell lines were grown in a humidified atmosphere containing \(5\%\,\mathrm{CO_{2}}\) in the DMEM (high glucose) at \(37\ ^{\circ}{\mathrm{C}},\) respectively. After cultivation for two days, the cells were washed with PBS and digested for \(2\,\mathrm{min}\) by adding \(1\,\mathrm{mL}\) of trypsin. The digestion was stopped by adding \(5~\mathrm{mL}\) of DMEM, and the resulting cells were centrifuged and dispersed in \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) of DMEM. Then, the numbers of LO2, MCF7 and HeLa cells were measured with a cytometer, respectively. Above 5 \(\times\,10^{6}\) cells were harvested and centrifuged at \(630\,g\) for \(5\,\mathrm{min}\) to remove the supernatant. The total raw RNA (RNAi) extraction of LO2, MCF-7 and HeLa cells was performed using an UNIQ-10 Column Trizol Total RNA Isolation Kit (Sangon Biotech Co., Ltd., China). After homogenizing the pellet for \(5\;\mathrm{min}\) in Tri Reagent at room temperature, the extractions were carried out with chloroform. Finally, the RNAi of LO2, MCF-7 and HeLa cells were precipitated with isopropanol, followed by washing twice with \(70\%\) EtOH and storing at \(-80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in RNase-free water for the latter assays.
## 2.4. Fabrication of electrochemical biosensor for miRNA-21 detection
First, the \(2\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of miRNA-21 with gradient concentrations and \(5\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of H2 \((2\,\upmu\mathrm{M})\) were dropped on the above fabricated H1/AuNPs/GOx@ZIF8 modified GCE, followed by incubation at \(4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\;\mathrm{{h}}\) . Then, \(6~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of hemin \((5~\upmu\mathrm{M})\) was added and incubated for \(^{1\mathrm{~h~}}\) to form the G-quadruplex/hemin HRP-mimicking DNAzyme. After thoroughly rinsed with ultrapure water, the electrode was further incubated in PBS \(_{(0.1\ M}\) containing \(150\ \mathrm{mM\NaCl}\) , \(\mathsf{p H}\,7.0\AA\) ) that contains glucose \(\left(1\mathrm{\mM}\right)\) , followed by adding \(200~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of ABTS \(\left(20\ \mathrm{mM}\right)\) and incubating for \(5\;\mathrm{min}\) at
\(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . Finally, the DPV signals were recorded on an electrochemical workstation (the scan range: \(0.2\;\mathrm{V}{-}0.7\;\mathrm{V}\) , pulse width: \(50\;\mathrm{ms}\) , scan rate: \(50\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) , pulse period: 0.2 s).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of AuNPs/GOx@ZIF-8 nanoreactors
