## 3.2. Feasibility of electrochemical biosensor
To verify the stepwise modification of GCE (Fig. 2A), the cyclic voltammetry (CV) and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) were performed in \(\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]^{3-/4}}\) \(\left(5\mathrm{~mM}\right)\) solution containing KCl (0.1 M) at a scan rate of \(50\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\). As shown in Fig. 2B, compared with the bare GCE (curve a), the peak current is decreased after modification of GOx@ZIF-8 (curve b) due to the relatively poor electrical conductivity of ZIF-8 (Paul et al., 2018). However, the AuNPs/GOx@ZIF-8 modified electrode displays a higher redox peak current (curve c), indicating the high conductivity of AuNPs which can accelerate the electron transfer at the interface of electrode. When the DNA orbits (H1) are connected to the AuNPs/GOx@ZIF-8 nanoreactors through Au–S bond, the redox peak current is decreased on account of the electrostatic repulsion
between negatively charged H1 and \(\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]^{3-/4}}\) (curve d). Upon the introduction of target miRNA-21, the 3D DNA walker is triggered with the assistance of H2, resulting in the formation of H1/H2 hybrid on electrode. Since H2 contains the G-rich fragment which can form G-quadruplex/hemin DNAzyme with HRP-mimicking activity, the redox peak current is decreased significantly under the electrostatic repulsion between negatively charged DNA and \(\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]^{3-/4}}\) (curve e) (Bai et al., 2019). Moreover, the variation of electron transfer rate on the electrode interface during fabrication procedures was proved by EIS characterization (Fig. 2C). The parameter (Ret) in the inserted equivalent circuit can be read from the corresponding Nyquist diagrams, where the diameters of semicircles are related to the charge-transfer resistance of [Fe \((\mathrm{CN})_{6}]^{3-/4}\) redox couple at the electrode interface. After modification of GCE with GOx@ZIF-8, the Ret value is increased (curve b), indicating that the electron transfer can be hampered with the poor electroconductivity of ZIF-8 (Bai et al., 2019). In order to improve the electroconductivity, the AuNPs were decorated on GOx@ZIF-8. As expected, the Ret is reduced obviously in comparison with GOx@ZIF-8 modified electrode (Fig. 2C, curve c), demonstrating that the AuNPs can promote the electron transfer on the electrode interface. When H1 is linked to the
AuNPs/GOx@ZIF-8 nanoreactors by Au–S bond, the Ret is increased owing to the electrostatic repulsion between the negatively charged H1 and \(\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]^{3-/4-}}\) (curve d). After the performance of 3D DNA walker triggered by target miRNA-21, the Ret is increased dramatically due to the formation of H1/H2 hybrids, which further hinders the charge transfer on the electrode surface (curve e) (Zhu et al., 2017). Overall, the above results confirm the successful modification of GCE step wisely and preliminarily verify the feasibility of 3D DNA walker triggered by target miRNA.
## 3.3. Native polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (PAGE) characterization of DNA walker
To further characterize the feasibility of the proposed DNA walker which can be triggered by target miRNA-21, the non-denaturing PAGE is carried out (Fig. 2D). In the absence of target miRNA-21, H1 and H2 are presented in a metastable state, which thus cannot react with each other (lane 5). After addition of miRNA-21 in H1, a band with slow migration is appeared (lane 6), indicating that the hairpin structure of H1 can be opened by miRNA-21 to form a H1/miRNA-21 duplex. When miRNA-21
is mixed with H1 and H2 (lane 7), bands migrated slower than that in lane 6 are produced, proving the successful implementation of DNA walker initiated by miRNA-21 with the formation of H1/H2 hybrids based on the strand displacement reaction. In addition, the released miRNA-21 can be clearly observed in lane 7, which further demonstrate the recycling of miRNA-21 during DNA walking.
## 3.4. Cascade catalytic performance of DNAzyme/AuNPs/GOx@ZIF-8
To assess the catalytic performance of \(\mathrm{GOx@ZIF-8}\), the calibration curve of absorbance at \(595\;\mathrm{nm}\) vs GOx concentration is first obtained by colorimetric detection using a Bradford Protein Assay Kit (details are shown in Supporting Information) (Fig. 3A), and the concentration of GOx encapsulated in ZIF-8 is quantified as \(7.07\;\mathrm{mg/mL}\). From Fig. 3B, the substrate solution containing glucose, HRP-mimicking DNAzyme and ABTS shows a typical absorption peak of ABTS (curve a). When the free GOx \((7.07\;\mathrm{mg/mL})\) is added into the substrate, an absorption peak centered at \(418\;\mathrm{nm}\) is appeared (curve c), which can be ascribed to the formation of \(\mathrm{ABTS^{+}}\) in the cascade catalytic reaction. Compared with the free GOx, the \(\mathrm{GOx@ZIF-8}\) exhibits almost the uniform optical absorption (curve b), indicating the good maintained catalytic activity of GOx after embedding into ZIF-8 (Liu et al., 2019).
To explore the impact of proximity effect on the cascade catalytic reaction, the UV–vis kinetics curves of DNAzyme/AuNPs/GOx@ZIF-8 nanoreactor and the mixture of free DNAzyme and AuNPs/GOx@ZIF-8 nanoreactor were studied by the oxidization of ABTS, respectively (Fig. 3C–D). As expected, the DNAzyme/AuNPs/GOx@ZIF-8 nanoreactor exhibits a higher UV absorption at \(418\;\mathrm{nm}\) at the same time. The corresponding UV–vis absorption spectras of these two groups are also recorded after reaction for \(60\;\mathrm{min}\) (Fig. 3E), and the DNAzyme/AuNPs/ GOx@ZIF-8 nanoreactor are also demonstrated a higher catalytic activity based on the proximity effect in the biocatalytic cascade.
## 3.5. Optimization of experimental conditions
To achieve the best biosensing performance and detection efficiency of this electrochemical biosensor, some important experimental conditions, such as the concentration of AuNPs used in material preparation process, the reaction time of 3D DNA walker, the concentration of H1 and glucose concentration, were optimized. In this work, the AuNPs are loaded on the surface of ZIF-8 by electrostatic interaction, which can be used to not only anchor the orbit of 3D DNA walker by Au–S covalent bond but also promote the electron transfer on electrode interface. The numbers of AuNPs coated on ZIF-8 directly affects the assay performance, which thus has been optimized. First, the H1/AuNPs/GOx@ZIF-8 modified GCE with different concentrations of AuNPs was used to detect miRNA-21 \((1\;\upmu\mathrm{M})\). As shown in Fig. 4A, the DPV signal increases with the increased AuNPs concentration due to the high electroconductibility of the sensing interface accomplished by AuNPs. When the concentration of AuNPs is higher than \(1.2\;\mathrm{mM}\), the DPV signal decreases because the superabundant AuNPs on ZIF-8 surface can block the contact between substrates and enzymes, which further impedes the cascade catalysis reaction. It should be noted that with further increasing the AuNPs concentration (from \(1.2\;\mathrm{mM}\) to \(2.5\;\mathrm{mM}\)), the DPV signal keeps stable owing to the saturation adsorption of AuNPs on ZIF-8 surface. Therefore, the optimal concentration of AuNPs for preparing the ZIF-8@AuNPs nanoreactor is selected as \(1.2\;\mathrm{mM}\). We further carried out optimization experiments about the concentration of H1. As shown in Fig. 4B, with increasing of the concentration of H1, the peak current is enhanced since more and more HRP-mimicking DNAzyme-labeled DNA probe can be immobilized on the electrode via hybridization with H1. The maximum peak current is obtained when the concentration of added H1 is \(2\;\upmu\mathrm{M}\), which thus selected as the optimal concentration for H1 immobilization. Subsequently, as shown in Fig. 4C, the peak current is enhanced with increasing reaction time of DNA walker, which can be ascribed to the formation of G-quadruplex/hemin DNAzyme via DNA walker that can catalyze the oxidation of ABTS by \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to form ABTS
radical cation and produce the electrochemical signal (Ponlakhet et al., 2019). The maximum peak current can be obtained when the incubation time is \(140\;\mathrm{min}\), which thus is selected as the optimal reaction time for the operation of 3D DNA walker. In addition, glucose is a vital factor in this study to perform the cascade catalytic reaction. Thus, the concentration of glucose is investigated. As illustrated in Fig. 4D, the peak current achieves the maximum when \(10\;\mathrm{mM}\) glucose participates in the cascade catalytic reaction. Thus, \(10\;\mathrm{mM}\) glucose is chosen as the optimal concentration in the detection system.
## 3.6. Detection of miRNA-21 using the proposed electrochemical biosensor
Under the optimal experimental conditions, the constructed label-free electrochemical biosensor was employed to detect miRNA-21 with various concentrations by DPV. As shown in Fig. 5A, the DPV signal increases with the elevation of miRNA-21 concentration. When the concentration of miRNA-21 ranges from \(0.1\;\mathrm{nM}\) to \(10\;\upmu\mathrm{M}\), the DPV signals demonstrate a good linear relationship with a detection limit of \(29\;\mathrm{pM}\) (3S/N) (Fig. 5B). Furthermore, the selectivity of the developed electrochemical biosensor was studied by the determination of miRNA-21, single-base mismatched miRNA-21, three-base mismatched miRNA-21 and miRNA-155. As shown in Fig. 5C, since miRNA-21 is perfectly complementary to H1 (the orbits of 3D DNA walker), the strongest DPV signal is produced by miRNA-21, which exhibits the prominent selectivity of the proposed electrochemical biosensor to distinguish target miRNA from the mismatched ones, even single-base mismatched miRNA. In addition, a relative standard deviation (RSD) of \(5.1\%\) was obtained from three different biosensors using H1/AuNPs/GOx@ZIF-8 modified GCE as working electrodes which were parallelly prepared in the same way for the determination of miRNA-21 at the same concentration \((1\;\upmu\mathrm{M})\), demonstrating great reproducibility and displaying great potential in clinical diagnosis. What's more, the storage stability of the H1/AuNPs/GOx@ZIF-8 modified GCE has been investigated. As shown in Fig. S5, the six parallel H1/AuNPs/GOx@ZIF-8 modified GCEs were stored for 0, 4, 8, 12, 16 and 20 days in a refrigerator at \(4\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), respectively. Then, these electrodes with different storage times were used to determine miRNA-21 at the same concentration \((1\;\upmu\mathrm{M})\). After storage for
20 days at \(4\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the peak current of H1/AuNPs/GOx@ZIF-8 sensing interface is remained \(90.56\%\) of the initial response, which indicates the good storage stability of H1/AuNPs/GOx@ZIF-8 modified GCE.
## 3.7. Real sample analysis
Thus, to verify the practical usability and reliability of the proposed electrochemical biosensor, different concentrations of miRNA-21 were detected using the standard addition method. To reduce the matrix effect, the normal serum samples are diluted 100-fold for analysis. Moreover, the dilution medium (PBS) is consistent with that used in standard detection, which may make the results more robust and accurate (Zhu et al., 2017). The recoveries of \(92\%{-}108\%\) and RSDs less than \(5.3\%\) are achieved, demonstrate that the system has good rate of recovery and excellent reproducibility (Table S2). Further, the amount of miRNA-21 in total RNA (RNAi) extracted from human normal liver cell (LO2), breast cancer cells (MCF-7) and cervical cancer cells (HeLa) were determined. The total RNAs extracted from LO2, HeLa, and MCF-7 cells \((1\times10^{6}\) cells) were quantified to be 23.82, 48.74 and \(85.94\;\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}\) using microplate reader, respectively. As shown in Fig. 5D, according to calibration curve method, miRNA-21 were successfully found in cancer cells of HeLa and MCF-7, and the concentrations of miRNA-21 are calculated to be 3.07 and \(7.34\;\mathrm{nM}\), respectively. In LO2, miRNA-21 could not be detected which might resulted from their ultralow concentration in the cells. Thus, the copy numbers of miRNA-21 per cells are calculated to be 1680 and 4018 copies/cell in HeLa and MCF-7 cells, respectively, according to the formula \(\mathbf{n}=(\mathbf{M}\times\mathbf{V})\times6.02\times10^{23}/\mathbf{N}\), where M is the concentration of miRNA-21, V is the volume, and N is the cell numbers. These results are in accordance with the results reported in previous works (Dodgson et al., 2012) and shown a good agreement with the results obtained by qRT-PCR (Table S3). All the above results confirm the great practicability of the proposed electrochemical biosensor for target miRNA detection in complex biological environments, which is promising for sensitive and selective analysis of miRNA in clinical applications.
## 4. Conclusion
4. ConclusionIn summary, a label-free electrochemical biosensor integrated the merits of ZIF-8 nanoreactors and 3D DNA walker is constructed for amplification detection of miRNA. The 3D DNA walkers can be triggered by the target miRNA and achieve the self-sufficient operation to facilitate the cascade catalysis reactions between GOx and HRP-mimicking DNAzyme based on the proximity effect, resulting in an enhanced electrochemical readout. As the support of 3D DNA orbits, the AuNPs are also employed as the bridge for electron transport to further improve the detection sensitivity. Given the high sensitivity and specificity, this ZIF-8 nanoreactors-based electrochemical biosensor is successfully applied to the detection of miRNA in real samples, which exhibits the potential applications in clinical diagnosis and opens a promising avenue to the development of DNA nanotechnology in biosensing fields.
